      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A patient underwent successful percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA) of the left anterior descending and the right coronary arteries for worsening angina and was discharged without any apparent complication. Repeat cardiac catheterization, done four weeks later for recurrent angina, showed a coronary artery fistula to the right ventricle at the site of moderately severe subintimal dissection. The patient was managed conservatively. At cardiac catheterization 18 months after the PTCA procedure, there was no evidence of the fistula."},
